   28: #  *** THE GUARDS ARE WRONG, NOT MERELY HARSH, AND THE MANUAL IS WHAT SAYS SO. ***  Every `legal'
   29: #  row below drives a value the 21066/21066A HRM documents as legal for that register, and each
   30: #  one cites its own line in `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt'.  The sharpest is W_BASE1: S6.4.14
   31: #  item 3 (:10791-10792) says SROM "Prevents the IOC from responding to itself by writing zero to
   32: #  the IOC_W_BASE1 and IOC_W_BASE0 registers".  ZERO IS THE VALUE THE INITIALISATION SEQUENCE IS
   33: #  REQUIRED TO WRITE, and at W_BASE1 it ends the emulator.  It is also the value the model's own
   34: #  register reads back before anyone writes it, so writing back what you just read is fatal.
   35: #
   36: #  THE MANUAL VALUES USED, each verified by reading the cited lines rather than taken from a seat:
   37: #
   38: #    CONF    -> 2      Table 6-10 (:10246-10254): bits <1:0> are the CFG AD<1:0> FIELD, so all
   39: #                      four encodings are legal.  S6.2.9 (:9217) says the same from the other end.
   40: #                      The guard accepts HALF of a documented two-bit field.
   41: #    TB_ENA  -> 0x81   Table 6-13 (:10443, :10453): bits 63:8 and 6:0 are RES of type X, and the
   42: #                      manual's own type legend (:985-988) defines X as "return UNPREDICTABLE
   43: #                      results when read and are IGNORED ON WRITE OPERATIONS".  0x81 differs from
